WAIT(2)                        P32W Unix                         WAIT(2)

NAME
       wait - wait for a child process to stop or terminate

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/wait.h>

       pid_t wait(int *status);

       WIFEXITED(s)    WEXITSTATUS(s)
       WIFSIGNALED(s)  WTERMSIG(s)
       WIFSTOPPED(s)   WSTOPSIG(s)

DESCRIPTION
       wait suspends the caller until one of its children has
       terminated, or, for a child being traced with ptrace(2),
       has stopped. If a child has already done so, wait returns at
       once. A terminated child is reaped: its process slot is
       freed and its CPU times, and those of its own reaped
       children, are added to the caller's children's times
       (times(2)). A stopped child is reported once per stop.

       If status is not a null pointer, the child's status is
       stored there:

       exited       the exit code (exit(2)) times 256: bits 8-15
                    hold it, bits 0-7 are 0.
       killed       the number of the signal that killed it, in
                    bits 0-6; bits 8-15 are 0. No core is dumped,
                    so bit 7 is never set.
       stopped      0177 in bits 0-7 and the signal that stopped
                    it in bits 8-15 (traced children only).

       The macros of <sys/wait.h> take the status apart:
       WIFEXITED(s) is true if the child exited, and then
       WEXITSTATUS(s) is its exit code; WIFSIGNALED(s) is true if a
       signal killed it, and WTERMSIG(s) is the signal;
       WIFSTOPPED(s) is true if it stopped, and WSTOPSIG(s) is the
       signal that stopped it.

RETURN VALUE
       The process ID of the child; -1 on an error, with errno
       set.

ERRORS
       ECHILD  The caller has no children (or none left).
       EINTR   A signal arrived while the caller waited.
       EFAULT  status is a bad address. (The child has been
               reaped all the same.)

SEE ALSO
       exit(2), fork(2), ptrace(2), signal(2), sh(1)

BUGS
       There is no waitpid, wait3 or WNOHANG: a program cannot
       wait for one particular child, or poll.
